# KNOWN GAP (filed 2026-08-06, sibling of the Round XXXII opaque-handle
# receiver-ABI class fix): `Vector[T]` where T is a SelfConvention::ByValue
# opaque-handle protocol (AtomicInt, Mutex, RWLock, Semaphore, Barrier, ...)
# SEGVs on both C and LLVM backends. gg check ACCEPTS it.
#
# INTENDED: prints `2`, exit 0.
#
# ROOT CAUSE (measured 2026-08-06 -- DIFFERENT LAYER from the Round XXXII
# receiver-ABI chokepoint fix). An opaque handle IS a pointer, so pushing
# an `AtomicInt` (which is a pointer VALUE) into a `Vector[AtomicInt]`
# passes the handle POINTER as `const void* elem` to `gorget_array_push`:
#
#   __v5 = gorget_atomic_int_new(0);     // handle = heap pointer
#   gorget_array_push(__v3, __v5);       // memcpy(slot, __v5, 8)
#
# `push` treats `elem` as pointer-to-value-to-copy, so `memcpy(slot, handle, 8)`
# reads the FIRST 8 bytes of the AtomicInt struct (the `__val` field, initial
# 0) into the slot -- NOT the handle pointer itself. The slot now stores `0`
# and the original handle is leaked. Every subsequent `v[0].add(1)` reads the
# slot value (`0`) and dispatches to `atomic_int_add(0, 1)` -> SEGV.
#
# The chokepoint fix at `methods.rs:2315-2337` (Round XXXII) correctly
# LOADS the slot value and passes it as the receiver -- so the receiver-
# ABI half of the class is right. The SLOT itself is corrupt because push
# stored 8 bytes of the wrong memory. The push-side arg-building for
# scalar-sized value types (bool, int, and by-value opaque handles) is what
# emits `&(int64_t){__v5}` for int -- it needs an equivalent `&__v5` wrap
# for opaque-handle receivers. Owner-decided fix layer is a follow-up scout
# in a subsequent round; graduating this cell is +1 close then.
#
# CLASS. Vector[AtomicInt] (this fixture) and Vector[Mutex[int]] (sibling
# `vector_mutex_elem_lock_segv.gg`) are the two measured members of the
# same class; the other 10 by-value protocols share the mechanism per the
# receiver-ABI class taxonomy in `mutex_amp_param.gg`.

from std.sync import AtomicInt

void main():
    Vector[AtomicInt] v = Vector[AtomicInt]()
    v.push(AtomicInt(0))
    v[0].add(1)
    v[0].add(1)
    print(v[0].load())
